From 1b98ecceb18e63d60d7d86de9ed1575ae3c2cfd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 18:48:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 71/71] inline-3d(#92): re-negotiate out of degraded weave,
 keep the batch shape
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two things go wrong when the weave client races a displayxr-service restart at
startup, and #92 saw them together. Only one of them was where it looked.

The submit SHAPE never actually depended on the rig extensions — SubmitBatchWin
gates on the negotiated weave spec version alone, and the weave-only retry
leaves that untouched. What it depends on is whether a session exists AT ALL in
the GPU process: DisplayXRWeaveGpu::SyncMode() is literally IsSupported(), and
BatchMode() is SyncMode() && spec >= 3. InitializeCore latched |initialized| =
true on its very first line and never cleared it, so one transient
xrCreateInstance / xrCreateSession failure left the session NULL for good — and
the GPU process gets exactly one attempt, pre-sandbox, because the runtime IPC
pipe, the registry and OpenProcess are all out of reach after LowerToken. Viz
then falls back to the browser-process async mojo bridge, and
DisplayXRWeaverImpl::WeaveSubmit can only carry ONE rect per call. That is the
reported signature: rect_count == 0, one submit per element per frame, zero
"batch weave: n=" lines, and a runtime DP write scoped to each element rect
instead of the window — which is what leaves the never-cleared weave output
showing stale pixels at every earlier scroll position, and what feeds the vendor
predictor badly (#96).

So the shape fix is to make a transient failure retryable rather than fatal. An
init attempt now reports kOk / kTransient / kUnavailable: kTransient (instance,
system, graphics-requirements or session refused by a service that is coming
back) releases everything it built and clears the latch; kUnavailable (no
loader, no weave extension, no D3D11 device on the runtime's adapter) re-latches,
because no retry can change a machine fact. The GPU pre-sandbox path retries a
transient failure 4 times at 1s — bounded, on the failure path only, and cheap
against a watchdog budget the 8s window wait already spends more of — and the
browser path folds transient retries into the 500ms window-lookup chain it
already had. The invariant that was true by accident is now written down at the
gate that must keep it: batch depends on the weave spec version and nothing
else, not the rig, not |has_rig_views|, not degraded-ness.

The second half is that degraded meant FOREVER. A client that came up weave-only
never asked again, so every inline-3D scene stayed mono for the life of the
browser even though the service was healthy again seconds later. It now
re-negotiates on a backoff: 2s, 5s, 15s, 60s, then every 5 minutes. Service
restarts resolve in seconds, which is where essentially every real recovery
happens; an SR-stack or plug-in reinstall takes minutes; past that it is not a
race at all, and a five-minute tail costs one instance create/destroy per 300s
while making "install the new runtime, keep browsing" work without a restart.

Two independent brakes stop it retrying a runtime that genuinely lacks the
feature. If the original rejection is XR_ERROR_EXTENSION_NOT_PRESENT /
FEATURE_UNSUPPORTED / API_VERSION_UNSUPPORTED, the retry is never armed — that
runtime will not change its mind. And every attempt first re-enumerates
(instance-free) and switches off permanently if the rig names are simply not
there, which also covers a runtime downgraded under a running browser. The
observed -2 (XR_ERROR_RUNTIME_FAILURE) describes the far side, not the feature,
so it retries.

Each attempt PROBES before it swaps: a throwaway full instance, same extension
list as the real create (CreateWeaveInstance is shared so the two cannot drift),
no session, no device, no window bind. Only on success is the degraded session
torn down and rebuilt, and the swap is safe because every browser-process
toucher of the client state is UI-thread-sequenced — the init tasks, this task,
DisplayXRWeaverImpl::WeaveSubmit (its bind is posted to the UI thread),
DisplayXRServiceImpl's rig locates, the display-mode controller and the drag
subclass — so nothing runs between the teardown and the re-init. Sticky state is
transferred explicitly and enumerated in the code: the bound HWND, the phase-snap
reference (reset so the new DP is told where the window is), 0069's
latched_screen_flat memo (the runtime's copy died with the session, ours is
re-asserted verbatim), the browser#55 panel hardware state, and the per-session
mode-requestable answer, which is re-resolved rather than carried. The woven and
fence handles are deliberately NOT carried: the new session re-exports on its
first submit and Viz re-imports, on the path that already exists.

The GPU process does not do this and cannot — there is nothing to swap to behind
the sandbox. Its defence is the bounded pre-sandbox retry, which is the half that
protects the submit shape; re-negotiation protects the rig.

For field triage the mode is now greppable as [DisplayXR][weave-mode]: a one-shot
WARN on entering degraded that names the missing capabilities, the consequence
(scenes render mono), the weave spec version batching still keys off, and whether
the retry is armed or disabled; an INFO on recovery with how long degraded
lasted. There is no DisplayXR section in chrome://gpu to hook — gpu/config and
content/browser/gpu carry no DisplayXR plumbing at all — so this is log-only
rather than inventing a GPUInfo field for it.

Validated with per-TU clang-cl -fsyntax-only on the edited TU (clean; the checker
was tripwire-tested with an injected typo and reported the error), and the patch
was apply-checked against an independent 151 baseline carrying the series through
0070 — clean, no rebase drift. No build, no browser launch; hardware verification
is a supervisor/David eyeball against a service restart.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
